DESIGN MEMO

A hash table is the right data structure for this contact
management system because it allows fast lookups using a
contact's name as a key. Instead of searching through every
contact one at a time, the hash function calculates an index
where the contact should be stored. This makes searching,
inserting, and updating contacts O(1) on average.

I handled collisions using separate chaining with linked
lists. When two contact names produce the same index, the
new contact is added to the linked list at that position.
When searching, the program checks each Node in the linked
list until it finds the matching name or reaches the end.
I also included duplicate-key handling so that inserting a
contact with an existing name updates its phone number
instead of creating another entry.

An engineer might choose a hash table over a list when fast
lookups and frequent insertions are important. For example,
a contact management application needs to retrieve phone
numbers quickly, even when it stores hundreds or thousands
of contacts. A list would require checking multiple entries
to find a contact, resulting in O(n) search time. A tree
might be preferable when sorted data or ordered traversal
is needed, but a hash table is generally a better choice
when the main goal is fast access using unique keys.
